PASSWD(1)                      User Commands                       PASSWD(1)

NAME
    passwd - change a password

SYNOPSIS
    passwd [UID]

DESCRIPTION
    Change your own password, or, as the administrator, the password of
    the account UID. The new password is read without echo and stored as
    an Argon2id hash, which makes guessing it from a stolen account table
    slow; it lasts across reboots.

    UID is a number, not a name. One that does not parse is refused; it is
    never taken to mean your own account. passwd UID is how an account made
    by useradd(8) gets a password: useradd leaves it locked.

    Changing your own password also re-seals your own key slot, the one
    your old password opened, to the new one; no other account's slot is
    touched. If another account opened the volume at this boot and your
    account has no recorded slot, the change is refused rather than guessed:
    log in first after a restart and run passwd again.

SEE ALSO
    useradd(8)
